r observed dust radii, albeit within the uncertainties. Figure 6 (right panel) demonstrates how TUNER-fits to CO SLEDs of our targets reproduce their observed CO(1–0) line luminosities. We note HeLMS-54 as an exception; however, it has only galaxy-integrated CO(3–2) besides CO(1–0) data, suggesting a plausible improvement with more CO line observations. Overall, Group-1 targets are less distributed in both these parameter spaces than the Group-2 DSFGs. Figure 6 : (Left panel) Comparison of the ALMA-observed dust sizes to the characteristic radius inferred from the TUNER LVG modeling. As expected due to radiative transfer effects [ 14 , 10 ] , the LVG-derived radii are systematically larger than the observed dust sizes in most targets. (Right panel) Observed CO(1–0) line luminosities compared with those fitted by TUNER. Our best-fit models fit the observed CO(1–0) line luminosities within the error bars, with a median underestimation of ∼ \sim 0.10 dex. HeLMS-54 is an exception with TUNER underestimating its L CO ⁡ ( 1 − 0 ) , obs ′ L^{\prime}_{m CO(1-0),obs} by ∼ \sim 0.3 dex. In both panels, blue markers represent Group-1 targets, and orange triangles represent Group-2 sources. Figure 7 : HerBS-51 ( z = 2.183 z=2.183 ) is shown as a representative example of the TUNER LVG modeling results. Upper panel: The black dashed curve represents the median best-fit model to the combined dust and CO SEDs, while the gray shaded region indicates the 1 σ \sigma confidence interval. Black points show the observed fluxes, and the colored dotted curves correspond to individual density components, scaled up for clarity. Comparable fits are obtained for the remaining 11 DSFGs (Appendix § \lx@sectionsign VII.5 ). Lower panel: (Left, Center) Fractional far-infrared multiwavelength dust luminosities, CO line luminosities, and molecular gas mass distributions as a function of gas density. (Right) A comparison of CO-level populations between HerBS-51 and the Milky Way center. We show an example of HerBS-51 ( z = 2.183 z=2.183 ) in Figure 7 to illustrate the TUNER LVG results, with the corresponding corner plot shown in Appendix VII.5 . Similar to this representative case, the median best-fit dust SEDs for all sources are consistent with the observed far-infrared continuum data. However, 4/12 sources exhibit one of their CO transitions with measured flux lying marginally outside the 1 σ \sigma spread of the median CO SLED model (Appendix VII.5 ). While TUNER underestimates CO(1–0) line flux for HeLMS-54 (only 2 CO line-SLED) and HerS-8 (undersampled Rayleigh-Jeans tail in dust SED), CO(4–3) is also underpredicted for HerBS-43b and HerBS-191. In addition, the CO(3–2) flux is marginally overpredicted for HerS-8. Comparing the TUNER-derived gas masses with those inferred from the dust-SED approach ( § \lx@sectionsign III.2.1 ) for these objects, however, we find agreement within the quoted uncertainties (Tables 3 and 6 ) for all sources. In the entire sample, HerBS-43a is the only source for which the TUNER analysis yields a higher gas mass ( 8.44 − 1.20 + 0.72 × 10 11 ​ M ⊙ {8.44}^{+0.72}_{-1.20}~	imes{10}^{11}~{m M}_{\odot} ) compared to the dust-based estimate ( 4.17 − 1.12 + 1.12 × 10 11 ​ M ⊙ {4.17}^{+1.12}_{-1.12}~	imes{10}^{11}~{m M}_{\odot} ). At the same time, the sizes agree within the error bars (see Figure 6 ). The noted mass difference does not arise from the adopted G/D ratio, which is fixed at 100 in both methods, but instead reflects the different ways in which the gas reservoir is constrained. In the TUNER LVG framework, the molecular gas mass is primarily linked to the CO excitation and the column density required to reproduce the observed CO line luminosities over the spatially resolved emitting region. With the emitting area independently constrained, the CO SLED effectively restricts the allowed range of column densities, leading to a plausibly higher inferred gas mass solution. For example, SLED of HerBS-43a (Appendix VII.5 ) has a significantly unconstrained peak, allowing denser density components to dominate the CO energy distribution. In contrast, the GMBB approach infers the dust mass directly from the far-infrared continuum without incorporating constraints from molecular line excitation or optical-depth effects in CO. For HerBS-43a, the joint dust and CO fit, therefore, suggests higher column densities, resulting in a molecular gas mass approximately a factor of two higher than that derived from the continuum-only analysis. Furthermore, TUNER-inferred CO abundances for our sources are consistent with Galactic values within the uncertainties. 8 8 8 The TUNER models yield [ CO 12 ] / [ H 2 ] [m{}^{12}CO]/[H_{2}] abundance ratios that are generally consistent with near-solar or solar values (Table 6 ). These estimates, however, are derived assuming fixed values of the G/D ratio and dust opacity normalization, κ ν \kappa_{
